ELIPP (p)  17.2.12  \pi/2K(m)  \pi/4K(m)
(For theta functions, see chapter 16.)
Elliptic Integral of the Third Kind
17.2.14
\Pi(n;\varphi\backslash a)=\int_{0}^{\infty}\left(1-n\sin^{2}\theta\right)^{-1}[1-\sin^{2}\alpha\sin^{2}\theta]^{-1/2}d\theta\quad Ifx=\sin\,(u|m),
17.2.15
\Pi(n;u|m)=\int_{0}^{\infty}\left(1-nt^{2}\right)^{-1}[(1-t^{2})(1-mt^{2})]^{-1/2}dt\quad
17.2.16
The Amplitude
17.2.17
\varphi=amu=arcsin\,(sn\,u)=arcsin\,x
can be calculated from Tables 17.5 and 18.
Dependence on the parameter m is denoted by a vertical stroke preceding the parameter, e.g., F(\varphi|m).
Together with the parameter we define the complementary parameter m_1 by
17.2.18
m+m_1=1
When the parameter is real, it can always be arranged, see 18.4, that 0\leq m\leq1.
The Modular Angle a
Dependence on the modular angle a, defined in terms of the parameter by 18.2.1, is denoted by a backward stroke \ preceding the modular angle, thus E(\varphi\backslash a). The complementary modular angle is \pi/2-a\) or 90deg-a according to the unit and thus m_1=\sin^2 (90deg-a)=\cos^2 a.
The Modulus k
In terms of Jacobian elliptic functions (chapter 16), the modulus k and the complementary modulus are defined by
17.2.19
k=ns\,(K+iK'), k'=dn K.
They are related to the parameter by k^2=m, k'^2=m_1.
Dependence on the modulus is denoted by a lemma preceding it, thus \Pi(n;u,k).
In computation the modulus is of minimal importance, since it is the parameter and its complement which arise naturally. The parameter and the modular angle will be employed in this chapter to the exclusion of the modulus.
The Characteristic n
The elliptic integral of the third kind depends on three variables namely (i) the parameter, (ii) the amplitude, (iii) the characteristic n. When real, the characteristic may be any number in the interval (-a, \infty). The properties of the integral depend upon the location of the characteristic in this interval, see 18.7.7.
Complete Elliptic Integrals of the First and Second Kinds
Referred to the canonical forms of 18.2, the elliptic integrals are said to be _complete_ when the amplitude is \frac{1}{3}\pi and so z=1. These complete integrals are designated as follows
17.3.1
\[[K(m)]=K=\int_{0}^{1}[(1-t^{2})(1-mt^{2})]^{-1/2}dt\qquad\qquad\qquad=\int_{0}^{\pi/2}(1-m\sin^{2}\theta)^{-1/2}d\theta\quad\]
17.3.2
K=F(\tfrac{1}{2}\pi|m)=F(\tfrac{1}{2}\pi\backslash a)
17.3.3
E[K(m)]=E=\int_{0}^{1}(1-t^{2})^{-1/2}(1-mt^{2})^{1/2}dt\qquad\qquad\qquad=\int_{0}^{\pi/2}(1-m\sin^{2}\theta)^{1/2}d\theta\quad\]
17.3.4
E=E[K(m)]=E(m)=E(\tfrac{1}{2}\pi\backslash a)
We also define
17.3.5
K'=K(m_1)=K(1-m)=\int_{0}^{\pi/2}(1-m_1\sin^{2}\theta)^{-1/2}d\theta\quad\]
17.3.6
K'=F(\tfrac{1}{2}\pi|m_1)=F(\tfrac{1}{2}\pi\backslash\tfrac{1}{2}\pi-a)
17.3.7
E'=E(m_i)=E(1-m)=\int_{0}^{\pi/2}(1-m_1\sin^{2}\theta)^{1/2}d\theta\quad\]
17.3.8
E'=E[K(m_1)]=E(m_i)=E(m_i)=E(\tfrac{1}{2}\pi\backslash\tfrac{1}{2}\pi-a)
K and iK' are the ''real'' and ''imaginary'' quarter-periods of the corresponding Jacobian elliptic functions (see chapter 16).

